# Task 6 re-review — Fix Round 5

Date: 2026-08-28  
Review base: `ae5d837`  
Review head: `f66abeaecb24f856504677d776194b1ce2a215e7`  
Disposition: **NEEDS FIXES**  
Open findings: **0 Critical, 2 Important, 1 Minor**

## Scope and threat model

This was a fresh read-only review of the complete Fix Round 5 section of
`task-6-report.md`, all 2,689 lines of `review-ae5d837..f66abea.diff`, every
open Round 4 item, the Task 6 brief, the approved design, and the relevant
current authorization, gateway, budget, audit, recovery, and focused-test code.
Static evidence was sufficient, so I did not run tests. I ran only read-only
repository/status/whitespace checks and wrote this ignored review report. No
provider, network, live pricing, real SDK, replay/parity, Docker, background
process, permanent ledger, or user authorization was used.

The requested threat calibration is applied. Untrusted model-authored policy
code runs in the isolated worker and cannot import controller internals. No
Critical finding relies on reflection, a private underscore method, or mutation
of private capability state. The former supported-public forgery is closed.
Important 1 concerns recovery from tampered durable evidence, an explicit
design stop condition, and is Important rather than Critical because no
supported public writer now creates the forged package. Important 2 is a
normal in-scope Task 6 `BaseException` phase failure reachable through an
injected dependency.

Round 5 substantially improves the security boundary: generic namespaces and
optimizer events are reserved; reservation/start events have trusted
AuthorizationLedger commitments; authorization verification now precedes
BudgetLedger restore; provider, budget, and event evidence is read and hashed
through one protected open; and role-derived LoopState is replayed exactly.
Two lifecycle/accounting gaps still prevent readiness.

## Finding Verdicts

| Round 4 item | Round 5 verdict | Evidence |
|---|---|---|
| Critical 1 — generic namespace/event forgery and gateway-origin authority | **PUBLIC FORGERY ADDRESSED; DURABLE ORIGIN PARTIAL, OPEN AS IMPORTANT 1.** Generic names beginning `provider-call`, `provider-budget`, or `optimizer-receipt` are rejected (`agent_loop.py:12091-12099`), and public `append_event` rejects every optimizer state or lifecycle code (`12229-12245`). The private typed path derives the state from the live lifecycle (`12247-12267`). AuthorizationLedger commits reserved/start stages (`core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:1455-1572`), and the supported-public attack test is meaningful (`tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py:5777-5964`). The commitment never binds the terminal provider, budget, or event digests, however. See Important 1. |
| Important 1 — verify authorization before BudgetLedger restore, with genuine retry | **ADDRESSED.** Recovery obtains the stored active reservation, derives untrusted audit facts without mutation, calls `verify_terminal_audit_receipt`, and only restores the returned verified image (`agent_loop.py:3957-3978`). The test snapshots every fresh-budget field, rejects the wrong authorization identity without mutation, restores genuine bytes, retries on the same ledger, and unlocks exactly plan 2 (`tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py:6488-6645`). |
| Important 2 — one protected identity for provider/budget/receipt evidence | **ADDRESSED for the durable evidence files.** `_read_protected_audit_artifact` performs direct-parent/path, `lstat`, regular-file, symlink/reparse, single-link, size, one-open/one-read, descriptor/path identity, and same-byte hash checks (`agent_loop.py:11895-11969`). Provider and budget fresh/existing/recovery branches use `_read_protected_json` (`12401-12438`, `12602`, `12700`, `12792`), and event replay uses the same primitive (`13373`). Hardlink, reparse, and deterministic second-open tests are targeted (`tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py:6648-6857`). `TerminalAuditReceipt` is a projection of the protected provider/event evidence, not a separate receipt file; after reconciliation it is stored in the trusted hash-chained AuthorizationLedger. |
| Important 3 — exact role-derived LoopState replay | **ADDRESSED.** Replay maps the sealed record role to exactly one state and rejects every relevant lifecycle event with another state (`agent_loop.py:12897-12903`). The fully rehashed investigator-to-author relabel test fails before restore (`tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py:6859-6941`). |
| Minor 1 — evidence/report accuracy | **PARTIAL; OPEN.** The corrected implementation and most command/count/resource claims match the inspected tests. The claim that the focused REDs cover all optimizer lifecycle states is broader than the parameterization. See Minor 1. |

## Actionable findings

### Important 1 — recovery can authenticate a rewritten terminal package because the trusted commitment stops at request start

**Confidence: 9/10.**

The new trusted `gateway_lifecycle` primitive contains the plan, authorization
reservation, budget reservation, manifest, audit run, lease, reserved-event
digest, and optional started-event digest
(`core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:1487-1505`). The normal-path test confirms
that the AuthorizationLedger tail is reservation, reserved commitment, started
commitment, reconciliation; there is no terminal commitment
(`tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py:5572-5587`).

Terminal publication happens after BudgetLedger reconciliation: the gateway
writes the provider/budget/terminal receipt package and only then calls
`AuthorizationLedger.reconcile_call` (`agent_loop.py:3857-3909`). A crash at
that existing recovery boundary therefore leaves authentic reserved/start
commitments but no trusted provider/event/budget digest. On restart,
`_require_gateway_lifecycle_commitment` compares the facts only to the
reserved/start lifecycle digest and compares only the original budget
*reservation* primitive (`core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:1387-1452`). It
does not bind `provider_record_sha256`, `budget_recovery_sha256`,
`terminal_event_sha256`, usage, terminal outcome/code, or payload digest.

Consequently, after a genuine request reaches terminal audit but crashes before
authorization reconciliation, a rewritten provider usage/cost, matching budget
recovery image, and matching terminal event can extend the genuine hash chain
from the authentic start event. Semantic replay accepts the self-consistent
package (`agent_loop.py:13004-13088`), the start commitment still matches, and
recovery restores the rewritten budget and publishes a reconciliation
(`3931-3984`). The next plan is then unlocked. The audit-local hashes prove
consistency, but the trusted ledger does not prove that the gateway produced
those terminal facts. This violates the approved design's requirement to stop
on evidence tampering and to close call/token/cost/audit records exactly
(`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-27-model-authored-pit-optimizer-design.md:453-465`,
`560-564`).

**Required fix:** add a private gateway-owned terminal commitment to the
AuthorizationLedger before terminal evidence can authorize restart. Bind the
exact authorization reservation, provider record digest, budget recovery
digest, terminal event digest, run/manifest/call/iteration/role, usage and
charge basis (or their canonical digest), outcome, payload digest, and terminal
code. Recovery and reconciliation must require this commitment. A crash before
the terminal commitment must remain fail-closed; it must not be auto-resealed
from audit-local hashes. Prefer making the trusted terminal commitment and
authorization reconciliation one idempotent transaction where possible.

**Exact fake/temp RED:** start from
`_task6_crash_after_terminal_audit` in a pytest `tmp_path`, with the injected
fake client and fake pricing. Preserve the genuine reserved and started events
byte-for-byte and preserve their existing AuthorizationLedger commitments.
Change the provider record from the genuine 100/50/150, `$0.001` usage to a
different closed authoritative 120/60/180, `$0.002` usage. Change the budget
recovery snapshot/reconciliation to match while keeping the genuine budget
reservation primitive. Change only the terminal event's provider digest,
budget digest, snapshot, and terminal hash; keep its genuine authorization ID,
reservation/start digests, lifecycle digest, role, and ordering. Reopen a fresh
AuditTrail, AuthorizationLedger, and zero BudgetLedger and call public
`recover_pit_optimizer_finalization`.

Require rejection before BudgetLedger restore or authorization reconciliation,
and require `reserve_call(..., plan2)` to remain fenced. Current code accepts
the rewritten image, charges `$0.002`, reconciles call 1, and makes plan 2
available. This is the missing companion to the Round 5 wrong-authorization
test: that test rewrites the reserved/start authorization identity too
(`tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py:6556-6587`), so the new start commitment—not
terminal origin—causes its expected rejection.

**Why Task 6, not Task 7:** Task 6 defines the durable receipt, trusted ledger,
semantic recovery, accounting restore, reconciliation, and next-plan unlock.
Task 7 cannot add gateway provenance after Task 6 has accepted audit-local
terminal hashes as authorization evidence.

### Important 2 — a start-commitment exception releases budget capacity but strands the active authorization reservation

**Confidence: 10/10.**

`_ensure_pit_optimizer_started_event` first persists the start event and stores
its digest on the lifecycle, then binds the AuthorizationLedger commitment
(`agent_loop.py:3665-3675`). The caller sets `possibly_sent = True` only after
that whole method returns (`4252-4253`). If the binding dependency raises a
`BaseException` after the event is durable, outer cleanup still constructs
`failed_before_send` facts because `possibly_sent` is false (`4500-4515`).

Finalization reconciles BudgetLedger before validating the lifecycle
(`3857-3887`). It therefore releases the budget reservation with zero usage,
then `_seal_pit_optimizer_lifecycle` rejects those before-send facts because a
start event exists (`3692-3697`). The original exception is preserved, but the
AuthorizationLedger retains its active call reservation while BudgetLedger has
released the corresponding reservation (`4534-4556`). A next call fails on the
active authorization reservation; restart recovery also has no terminal audit
record to consume. This is a concrete cross-ledger divergence and terminal
lease wedge at a newly introduced Round 5 persistence boundary.

The existing phase test covers authorization reservation, reservation-event
append, SDK construction, start-event append, SDK invocation, and response
processing (`tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py:4873-4991`). It does not inject at
either lifecycle-commitment append.

**Required fix:** treat the durable start-event existence as the conservative
request-start boundary during every cleanup, even if commitment binding raises
before the wrapper assigns `possibly_sent`. Validate/commit all terminal
preconditions before releasing or charging BudgetLedger, or otherwise ensure a
failure preserves matching active budget and authorization fences. For a
one-shot commitment exception after a durable start event, retry the exact
idempotent commitment during cleanup and close the call as uncertain with the
full reservation retained; do not relabel it before-send and do not transmit a
replacement request.

**Exact fake/temp RED:** extend the existing synchronous phase test with
`phase="start_commitment"`. Monkeypatch the injected AuthorizationLedger's
`_bind_gateway_lifecycle_commitment` wrapper so that, on the first call where
`lifecycle.started_event_sha256 is not None`, it calls the genuine method and
then raises `KeyboardInterrupt("start_commitment")`. Invoke the ordinary fake
gateway once. Require zero SDK calls, one uncertain/started terminal record,
full retained budget exposure, one matching authorization reconciliation, and
lease closure as `cancelled`. Reopen the ledgers/audit and assert idempotent
terminal verification and that no same-plan retry can send. Current code
returns the interruption with no terminal record or authorization
reconciliation, releases the BudgetLedger reservation, and leaves the active
authorization fence so both same-plan retry and plan 2 fail without a recovery
artifact.

**Why Task 6, not Task 7:** the event/commitment/`possibly_sent` boundary and
both cleanup transactions are entirely inside Task 6's one-shot gateway. Task
7 cannot observe this internal partial publication safely, and retrying at the
controller would violate Task 6's no-retry/no-disguised-retry contract.

### Minor 1 — the report overstates lifecycle-state test coverage

**Confidence: 10/10.**

`task-6-report.md:628-632` says the focused REDs cover all lifecycle
codes/states. The public append parameterization covers all five reserved event
codes but only one optimizer role state (`CALL_INVESTIGATOR`); the other rows
use ordinary `PREPARE` (`tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py:5807-5839`). The replay
test covers one investigator chain relabeled to author, not every exact
role/state permutation (`6859-6941`). The implementation itself uses the full
three-state set and a role-derived exact mapping, so this is an evidence/report
correction, not a code blocker. Say that the tests cover every lifecycle code,
one representative public optimizer state, and one cross-role semantic relabel,
with the remaining state closure established by the shared set/mapping logic.

## New Breakage

- Important 2 is a new Round 5 failure boundary introduced by the separate
  started-event and AuthorizationLedger commitment publications.
- Important 1 is an incomplete closure of durable gateway-origin authority. It
  is not the former supported-public capability forgery; that Critical path is
  closed under the calibrated threat model.
- No other new Critical or Important regression was found in grant/window
  durability, source/manifest authentication, pricing freeze lifecycle,
  canonical wire bytes, normal authoritative/overage/uncertain accounting,
  accepted post-publication semantics, role synchronization, or legacy
  schema-v1 provider compatibility.

## Requirement Coverage

| Task 6 requirement | Result |
|---|---|
| Hash-chain/exclusive-lock durability; atomic grant + window | **Meets; unchanged.** Authorization history remains exclusively locked, hash-chained, complete-image replaced, and replay checked. Audit file framing/hash replay remains intact. |
| Old 20/19 isolation; named grant; most-restrictive ceilings | **Meets; unchanged.** No path infers authorization or combines stale capacity. |
| Authenticated manifest/requirements/source scope; non-secret approval | **Meets.** Canonical manifest, reverse-diff/source identity, and plan provenance remain authenticated; approval values are not persisted. |
| One-shot run key; concurrency/reopen; no-spend lease open | **Partial.** Lease open and synchronized role consumption remain sound. The start-commit exception cannot reach an exact terminal reopen (Important 2). |
| Frozen Decimal R1 pricing identity and no post-freeze drift | **Meets.** Fresh gateway cache ownership, live reauthentication, and late callback mutation checks remain intact. |
| Next-call-only reservation; exact indexes/iterations/roles | **Partial on recovery.** Normal issuance is exact, but an uncommitted rewritten terminal can unlock plan 2 (Important 1). |
| Authoritative/overage/before-send/uncertain reconciliation | **Partial.** Ordinary paths remain closed. Start-commit failure is misclassified before-send, and rewritten terminal accounting can be restored (Important 1/2). |
| Complete conservative bytes/tokens/USD before SDK/ledger/start effects | **Meets.** Captured canonical bytes and authenticated frozen pricing still drive preflight before the first reservation or SDK effect. |
| BudgetLedger/AuthorizationLedger consistency under every exception/cancellation | **Does not yet meet.** Important 2 produces a released budget reservation plus active authorization reservation. |
| All-R1 profile; one request; `max_retries=0`; no healing/recovery | **Meets in the ordinary wrapper.** No alternate model, retry, healing, or generation recovery was introduced. |
| Iteration-aware closed provider/audit facts; legacy compatibility | **Partial.** Exact role state, closed records, and schema-v1 compatibility are intact; terminal origin is not committed across the recovery gap (Important 1). |
| Terminal lease closure; no disguised retry | **Partial.** Normal and recovered genuine terminals close exactly; the start-commit phase wedges without a terminal record (Important 2). |
| Content/credential/source/provider-response secrecy | **Meets.** Reviewed durable artifacts contain closed accounting/lifecycle/model/pricing/digest facts only. |
| Record-grant CLI cannot infer authority, combine capacity, or touch credentials | **Meets; unchanged.** The CLI remains an administrative recorder and has no provider path. |

## Test/Report Audit

- The Round 5 tests use pytest temporary roots, fake clients/responses, injected
  pricing, and synchronous local mutation only. No test uses a real provider,
  network, live pricing, permanent ledger, replay engine, Docker, or background
  worker.
- The public namespace/event test and supported-public attack are honest and
  close the prior Critical supported API path. No private capability forgery is
  counted against them.
- The verify-before-restore test is a genuine fresh-ledger/no-mutation/retry
  test. Its changed authorization ID also changes the committed reserved/start
  identity, so it does not exercise terminal-only provenance (Important 1).
- The hardlink parameterization covers both provider and budget files; the
  simulated reparse and deterministic old-second-open tests match the protected
  reader. There is no standalone optimizer receipt file: the receipt is derived
  from the protected provider/event package and later embedded in the trusted
  AuthorizationLedger reconciliation.
- The exact-state relabel test is honest for investigator-to-author replay.
- The reported `233 passed`, dedicated `20 passed`, Ruff, and compilation
  outputs were inspected as recorded evidence but were not independently rerun
  in this constrained review. The 20-test count matches the inspected
  parameterizations.

## Resource/Secrecy Audit

- This review performed code/diff/status reads and `git diff --check`, then
  wrote only this ignored SDD report. It ran no tests or temporary reproducers.
- It started no provider/network/live-pricing/SDK/replay/container/background
  action and created no authorization grant, lease, ledger, audit package, or
  other permanent state.
- No prompt, strategy source, raw provider response, request header, credential,
  API key, or operator approval content is added to the reviewed artifacts or
  this report.

## Out-of-Scope Observations

- The AuthorizationLedger is the trusted, locked, hash-chained origin anchor.
  Its `_read_records` path is not the Round 5 protected audit-artifact reader;
  direct arbitrary corruption of that trusted ledger was excluded by the
  calibrated threat model. Applying equivalent hardlink/reparse/descriptor
  identity checks there would be defense in depth, not the present blocker.
- Live provider/SDK compatibility remains intentionally untested at this safety
  boundary.
- Pre-Round-5 optimizer histories lacking the new gateway commitments correctly
  fail closed and may need explicit trusted migration/disposition.
- Private-name import/reflection by isolated policy code remains excluded. No
  severity depends on such access.
- Task 7 owns orchestration order. It cannot repair terminal provenance or
  cross-ledger exception atomicity after Task 6 has accepted or stranded the
  transition.

## Final Disposition

**NEEDS FIXES.** Round 5 closes the supported-public namespace/event forgery,
authorization-before-restore ordering, protected single-read evidence, and
exact role-state replay. Task 6 still needs (1) a durable gateway-owned terminal
commitment that authenticates the exact provider/budget/event package used by
restart and (2) cancellation-safe accounting across the started-event to
authorization-commitment boundary. Add the two focused fake/temp REDs above
and correct the lifecycle-state test claim before readiness.
